> is x + y commutative for u8 x and y?
  note: commutative: it(x, y) = it(y, x) everywhere?
  ucas: it(x: u8, y: u8) = x + y
  ucas: lhs(x: u8, y: u8) = it(x, y)
  ucas: rhs(x: u8, y: u8) = it(y, x)
  ucas: equiv lhs rhs
lhs and rhs are equal on the domain of lhs (exhaustive)
> is x injective for u8 x?
  ucas: it(x: u8) = x
  ucas: injective it
injective it:
  injective: yes (one-to-one)
  proved, exhaustive over all 256 points
> is x surjective for u8 x?
  ucas: it(x: u8) = x
  ucas: surjective it
surjective it:
  surjective onto its type u8 (0..255): yes
  256 distinct values (proved, exhaustive over all 256 points)
> is x bijective for u8 x?
  ucas: it(x: u8) = x
  ucas: bijective it
bijective it:
  injective: yes (one-to-one)
  proved, exhaustive over all 256 points
  surjective onto its type u8 (0..255): yes
  256 distinct values (proved, exhaustive over all 256 points)
  bijective: yes
> is x odd for u8 x?
  note: "odd": read as an odd function, f(-x) = -f(x), not an odd number for every input (say "an odd function" or "always odd")
  ucas: it(x: u8) = x
  ucas: parity it
parity of it:
  odd: f(-x) = -f(x)
  proved, exhaustive, with -x and -f(x) in u8 arithmetic (two's complement: -x is 2^w - x)
> is x even for u8 x?
  note: "even": read as an even function, f(-x) = f(x), not an even number for every input (say "an odd function" or "always odd")
  ucas: it(x: u8) = x
  ucas: parity it
parity of it:
  odd: f(-x) = -f(x)
  proved, exhaustive, with -x and -f(x) in u8 arithmetic (two's complement: -x is 2^w - x)
> is x always odd for u8 x?
  note: "odd": read as an odd function, f(-x) = -f(x), not an odd number for every input (say "an odd function" or "always odd")
  ucas: it(x: u8) = x
  ucas: parity it
parity of it:
  odd: f(-x) = -f(x)
  proved, exhaustive, with -x and -f(x) in u8 arithmetic (two's complement: -x is 2^w - x)
> is x + 1 monotonic for u8 x?
  ucas: it(x: u8) = x + 1
  ucas: monotonic it
monotonic it:
  not monotonic on 0..255
  maximal intervals of monotonicity:
    x in 0..254: increasing
    x in 254..255: decreasing
  witness: x = 0 < x = 1 but f(0) = 1 < f(1) = 2 (not decreasing)
  witness: x = 254 < x = 255 but f(254) = 255 > f(255) = 0 (not increasing)
  note: without wrapping (as int) it is strictly increasing; the machine type wraps: f(254) = 255, f(255) = 0
  proved, exhaustive over all 256 points (forward difference f(x+1) - f(x))
